1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,728
Something broke at three in the morning.

2
00:00:02,748 --> 00:00:05,722
The answer is almost always written down, in a log.

3
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:09,596
The skill is knowing where to look, and how to read what you find.

4
00:00:10,066 --> 00:00:14,249
In this video: locate and interpret system log files and journals.

5
00:00:18,769 --> 00:00:21,352
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:21,822 --> 00:00:25,087
Locate and interpret system log files and journals.

7
00:00:25,557 --> 00:00:30,166
We'll find the log files, read the journal with journalctl, write our own message,

8
00:00:30,186 --> 00:00:31,916
and read a line piece by piece.

9
00:00:41,436 --> 00:00:44,258
A Linux system keeps logs in two places.

10
00:00:44,278 --> 00:00:47,952
The journal is kept by the service systemd-journald,

11
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:51,078
and you read it with the command journalctl.

12
00:00:51,098 --> 00:00:56,264
And rsyslog writes ordinary text files in the folder slash var slash log.

13
00:01:06,734 --> 00:01:10,046
The file messages holds general system messages.

14
00:01:10,066 --> 00:01:15,183
The file secure holds logins and sudo. The file cron holds scheduled jobs.

15
00:01:22,703 --> 00:01:26,593
Let us look. ls shows the content of slash var slash log.

16
00:01:26,613 --> 00:01:30,708
The pipe and head dash n twelve keep the first twelve lines.

17
00:01:30,728 --> 00:01:35,440
We see boot dot log, cron, the dnf logs, and a folder called journal.

18
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:47,343
rpm dash q rsyslog says the package is installed, so the text files exist.

19
00:01:47,363 --> 00:01:51,912
The journal folder exists too, so the journal is kept on disk.

20
00:01:51,932 --> 00:01:56,333
And ls on messages and secure confirms both files are there.

21
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:11,528
Now the journal. journalctl dash u picks one unit.

22
00:02:11,548 --> 00:02:16,554
Here the unit is kdump, and dash n three shows the last three lines.

23
00:02:16,574 --> 00:02:20,249
The option no pager prints straight to the screen.

24
00:02:28,719 --> 00:02:30,832
Dash k shows only kernel messages.

25
00:02:39,302 --> 00:02:44,716
Dash p err keeps only errors, and dash b means this boot only. The answer here is:

26
00:02:44,736 --> 00:02:48,626
no entries. A clean boot has no errors, which is good news.

27
00:02:58,146 --> 00:03:02,365
Now we read one line, from the unit sshd, which handles logins.

28
00:03:02,385 --> 00:03:05,594
First comes the time. Then the host name, node1.

29
00:03:05,614 --> 00:03:10,169
Then the program that wrote it, with its process number in brackets.

30
00:03:10,189 --> 00:03:12,121
And last, the message itself.

31
00:03:22,591 --> 00:03:26,887
This line says: sshd accepted a public key for the user student.

32
00:03:26,907 --> 00:03:30,056
So we know who logged in, from where, and when.

33
00:03:30,076 --> 00:03:32,619
That is what interpreting a log means.

34
00:03:40,139 --> 00:03:46,267
We can also write to the journal. logger dash t n26 adds a message with the tag n26.

35
00:03:46,287 --> 00:03:48,610
It prints nothing when it works.

36
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:59,358
journalctl dash t n26 finds it.

37
00:03:59,378 --> 00:04:04,030
And dash dash since five minutes ago limits the search by time.

38
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:07,812
This is how you find your own marker in a long log.

39
00:04:16,282 --> 00:04:20,730
And wc dash l counts the lines of this boot: over fourteen hundred.

40
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:23,131
A real search always needs a filter.

41
00:04:30,651 --> 00:04:34,087
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

42
00:04:35,607 --> 00:04:40,202
We reboot. Then journalctl dash dash list dash boots shows two boots:

43
00:04:40,222 --> 00:04:44,014
the old one is number minus one, and the new one is zero.

44
00:04:52,484 --> 00:04:55,160
And dash b minus one reads the previous boot.

45
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:58,157
Our message from before the reboot is still there,

46
00:04:58,177 --> 00:05:00,314
because the journal is kept on disk.

47
00:05:09,834 --> 00:05:13,901
Your turn. Write a message with logger and the tag ex200.

48
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:18,634
Save what journalctl finds for that tag in the file ex200 dot log.

49
00:05:18,654 --> 00:05:20,139
Then run the checker.

50
00:05:26,609 --> 00:05:28,360
Try before you open the solution.

51
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,951
Next up: preserve system journals.

52
00:05:36,421 --> 00:05:39,323
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
